In France, voting has traditionally been a low-tech experience: voters isolate themselves in a booth and deposit a pre-printed sheet indicating the candidate of their choice in an envelope.
After officers verify the identity of the voter, the voter enters the envelope into the ballot box and signs the ballot list.
French electoral legislation codifies procedures in a very rigorous manner.
Since 19888, the voting ballot boxes must be transparent, so that voters and observers can testify that no envelope is present at the beginning of voting and that no envelogue is added, with the exception of those of duly numbered and authorized voters.
Candidates can send representatives to observe all parts of the process. During the night, votes are counted by volunteers under careful supervision, following specific procedures.
