When a small group of living things (a small population) separates themselves from the main population from which they came (e.g., if they transpose a chain of mountains or a river, or if they go to an island from which it is not possible to return with easily), he often finds himself in a different environment from the one in which he lived before.
This new environment has different features and new competitors, so the new population will need to acquire characteristics different from those it already has and adapt to become a strong competitor.
The original population hasn't changed at all, they still need the same adaptations as before.
Over time, as the new population begins to adapt to its new environment, it becomes less and less similar to the other population.
Eventually, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations will look so different that they cannot be called of the same species.
We call this process of speciation, which simply means the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
