The founding of Taiwan dates back to the 15th century, when Europeans sailing around the region baptized the site as Formosa Island.
In 1624, the Dutch East Indies Company inaugurated a base in southwestern Taiwan, initiating a transformation into Aboriginal grain production practices and emplosing Chinese to work on its rice and sugar plantations.
In 1683, the forces of the Qing Dynasty (1644–1912) seized control of Taiwan's western and northern coastal areas and declared Taiwan as a province of the Qing Empire in 1885.
In 1895, after the defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894-1895), the Qing government signed the Treaty of Shimonoseki, by which it cedes sovereignty over Taiwan to Japan, which governs the island until 1945.
