The Great Pyramid of Giza is the only one of the seven wonders of the world that still exists these days.
Built by the Egyptians in the 3rd century B.C., the Great Pyramid is one of many large pyramid structures built to honor dead pharaohs.
Giza's Plateau, or "Necropolis of Giza", in the Egyptian Valley of the Dead, has several pyramids (among which the Great Pyramid is the largest), several small tombs, several temples and the great Sphinx.
The great pyramid was created in honor of Pharaoh Khufu, and many of the pyramids, tombs and smaller temples were built to honor Khufu's wives and relatives.
