The High Middle Ages was preceded by the Low Middle Ages and followed by the Late Middle Ages, which, by convention, ends around 1500.
Technological determinism is a term that encompasses a series of ideas in practice, from the supply of technology or the technological imperative to a strict notion that human destiny is determined by an underlying logic associated with scientific laws and their manifestation in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share 2 general ideas: the first is that technological development itself follows a path that goes beyond cultural or political influence; the second, is the idea that technology, in turn, has "effects" on societies that are inherent, rather than socially conditioned.
For example, someone can say that the car necessarily results in the development of roads.
However, a national road network is not economically viable for only a few cars, so new production methods are developed to reduce the cost of having an automobile.
Possession of mass cars leads to a higher incidence of accidents on the roads, which leads to the invention of new techniques in the health system to heal the injured bodies.
Romanticism had a great element of cultural determinism, drawn from writers such as Goethe, Fichte and Schlegel.
